--
^warning: ignoring
^CONVERSION ERROR$
inconsistent deduction
dropped the body
--
N5008 [stmt.if]/2: the condition of `if constexpr' is a contextually
converted constant expression of type bool; the discarded substatement does
not contribute to return type deduction ([dcl.spec.auto.general]/10).
[expr.const]/4: an element of a constexpr array is usable in constant
expressions.  A constexpr variable's reference is replaced by its
initializer, but a constexpr array stays an object (it must remain an
lvalue), so `kinds[1]' did not fold, both branches were type-checked and
deduction failed ("inconsistent deduction for the return type").  The
condition now folds through const objects' initializers as static_assert
does -- libc++'s `if constexpr (__cat == _None) return void(); else if
constexpr ...' in __get_comp_type.  Fixed in round 163b.
